<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Earldom of Orkney</h1><p class="st-meta">875 CE – 1472 CE (approx.) · capital Birsay</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Norse earldom of the Orkney and Shetland islands, the heart of a sea-based power in northern Britain for six centuries, recorded in the Orkneyinga saga.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Norse settlers took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> in the 9th century, displacing or absorbing the Pictish population. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkneyinga-saga">Orkneyinga saga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> granted the islands to Earl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rognvald-eysteinsson">Rognvald of Møre</a>, who passed them to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-eysteinsson">Sigurd</a>, the first earl, in the late 9th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The earls owed allegiance to the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and later also to the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>, but often acted independently. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thorfinn-the-mighty">Thorfinn the Mighty</a>, in the 11th century, ruled much of northern Scotland and built a minster at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birsay">Birsay</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlendsson">Earl Magnus</a>, murdered around 1117, was venerated as a saint, and his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rognvald-kali-kolsson">Rögnvald Kali Kolsson</a> began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-magnus-cathedral">St Magnus Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a> in 1137.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Norse line died out in 1231, Scottish families held the earldom, ending with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clan-sinclair">Sinclairs</a>. In 1468 Christian I of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway</a> pledged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iii-of-scotland">James III of Scotland</a> as security for his daughter Margaret's dowry, and in 1472 the earldom was annexed to the Scottish crown. Norse law and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norn-language">Norn language</a> lingered for centuries.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/earldom-of-orkney.jpg" alt="Earldom of Orkney" width="320" height="336"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Mann_and_the_Isles-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>875 CE – 1472 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Birsay</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thorfinn the Mighty, Magnus Erlendsson, Rögnvald Kali Kolsson</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse (Norn)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Orkney Earldom, Jarldom of Orkney</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1174&amp;focus=earldom-of-orkney" aria-label="Earldom of Orkney on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4610 189 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Earldom of Orkney lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4930 445L5225 425M4930 445L4892 500"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0M4892 500h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4930 445h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4930 445h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Birsay. <a href="/atlas?year=1174&amp;focus=earldom-of-orkney">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
